  "lyrics": [
    {
      "start": 0.05,
      "end": 3.93,
      "text": "A key is how you tell rows apart and how you point at them later.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 3.93,
      "end": 9.305,
      "text": "Start with a super key: any set of columns that is unique for every row.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 9.305,
      "end": 13.527,
      "text": "The whole row is a super key if no two rows are identical.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 13.527,
      "end": 15.222,
      "text": "That is a weak compliment.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 15.222,
      "end": 16.777,
      "text": "You want a smaller set.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 16.777,
      "end": 23.222,
      "text": "A candidate key is a super key with no leftover column you could drop and still be unique.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 23.222,
      "end": 27.083,
      "text": "email might be a candidate if you truly never repeat it.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 27.083,
      "end": 30.361,
      "text": "customer_id might be another candidate.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 30.361,
      "end": 33.388,
      "text": "You pick one candidate to be the primary key.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 33.388,
      "end": 35.208,
      "text": "The others can stay UNIQUE.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 35.208,
      "end": 40.111,
      "text": "A good primary key is unique, always present, and stable.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 40.111,
      "end": 44.763,
      "text": "Stable means it does not change because marketing renamed a product code.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 44.763,
      "end": 50.263,
      "text": "Natural keys come from the world: ISBN, email, government id.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 50.263,
      "end": 54.041,
      "text": "They are meaningful, and they change when the world changes.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 54.041,
      "end": 58.791,
      "text": "Surrogate keys are invented: an integer the database assigns.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 58.791,
      "end": 61.222,
      "text": "They are dull, and that is the point.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 61.222,
      "end": 63.625,
      "text": "They do not encode the customer's city.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 63.625,
      "end": 66.416,
      "text": "They do not change when the email changes.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 66.416,
      "end": 70.736,
      "text": "Prefer a surrogate for the primary key when the natural ones wiggle.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 70.736,
      "end": 74.458,
      "text": "Still put UNIQUE on the natural candidates you care about.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 74.458,
      "end": 79.833,
      "text": "ISBN should stay unique even if book_id is the primary key.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 79.833,
      "end": 81.833,
      "text": "Composite keys are allowed.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 81.833,
      "end": 85.444,
      "text": "order_id and line_no together.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 85.444,
      "end": 87.625,
      "text": "They are honest for weak entities.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 87.625,
      "end": 92.013,
      "text": "They are clumsy as foreign keys in every child of the child.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 92.013,
      "end": 96.277,
      "text": "Sometimes a surrogate on the line table is worth the extra column.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 96.277,
      "end": 98.944,
      "text": "Never use a name as a primary key.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 98.944,
      "end": 101.194,
      "text": "Never use a mutable status.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 101.194,
      "end": 106.125,
      "text": "Never use something you would be embarrassed to print on an invoice ten years later.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 106.125,
      "end": 108.25,
      "text": "Write down every candidate.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 108.25,
      "end": 110.333,
      "text": "Strike the ones that can be null.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 110.333,
      "end": 112.361,
      "text": "Strike the ones that change.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 112.361,
      "end": 116.125,
      "text": "Strike the ones that are too wide to copy into children.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 116.125,
      "end": 117.888,
      "text": "Pick the dullest remaining.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 117.888,
      "end": 122.708,
      "text": "That dull integer is how orders will find customers for the rest of the sketch.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 122.708,
      "end": 124.958,
      "text": "UUIDs are surrogates too.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 124.958,
      "end": 128.458,
      "text": "They spread better across shards, and they are wide.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 128.458,
      "end": 131.222,
      "text": "Integers are easier to type while you learn.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 131.222,
      "end": 134.652,
      "text": "Either is dull enough if you do not encode meaning in them.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 134.652,
      "end": 137.013,
      "text": "Do not put the year into the id.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 137.013,
      "end": 139.694,
      "text": "Do not put the table name into the id.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 139.694,
      "end": 141.902,
      "text": "Those facts belong in columns.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 141.902,
      "end": 145.708,
      "text": "Natural keys that look unique globally often are not.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 145.708,
      "end": 147.18,
      "text": "Email changes.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 147.18,
      "end": 149.041,
      "text": "Phone numbers are reused.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 149.041,
      "end": 152.847,
      "text": "National ids are sensitive and not always present.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 152.847,
      "end": 162.375,
      "text": "ISBN is one of the better natural keys, and still needs UNIQUE beside a surrogate if you also sell used copies as inventory rows.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 162.375,
      "end": 165.388,
      "text": "Inventory is a different entity from title.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 165.388,
      "end": 168.347,
      "text": "The key conversation will catch that if you let it.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 168.347,
      "end": 172.361,
      "text": "Composite primary keys make ORM tools grumpy.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 172.361,
      "end": 176.194,
      "text": "That is not a modeling reason, but it is a project reason.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 176.194,
      "end": 180.597,
      "text": "If you add a surrogate to please a tool, keep the composite UNIQUE.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 180.597,
      "end": 183.208,
      "text": "The tool should not erase the candidate.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 183.208,
      "end": 185.694,
      "text": "Document why the primary key won.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 185.694,
      "end": 189.069,
      "text": "A one-line comment in the schema file is enough.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 189.069,
      "end": 193.777,
      "text": "Future you will thank present you when someone wants to key on email again.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 193.777,
      "end": 196.972,
      "text": "Dull, present, stable, unique.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 196.972,
      "end": 199.402,
      "text": "Write those four words above the column.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 199.402,
      "end": 200.416,
      "text": "Then pick.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 200.416,
      "end": 204.444,
      "text": "A key you are proud of is often a key that encodes a story.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 204.444,
      "end": 205.902,
      "text": "Stories change.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 205.902,
      "end": 207.333,
      "text": "Dull keys do not.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 207.333,
      "end": 211.819,
      "text": "If you can say the key out loud as a sentence about the business, look again.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 211.819,
      "end": 215.944,
      "text": "customer_id 104 is not a sentence.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 215.944,
      "end": 221.5,
      "text": "AUSTIN-MAYA-2024 is a sentence, and a trap.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 221.5,
      "end": 223.486,
      "text": "Keep the story in columns.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 223.486,
      "end": 225.555,
      "text": "Keep the key empty of plot.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 225.555,
      "end": 228.347,
      "text": "UNIQUE the story if it must stay unique.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 228.347,
      "end": 230.208,
      "text": "Point children at the dull one.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 230.208,
      "end": 233.277,
      "text": "That split will still look wise in five years.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 233.277,
      "end": 238.027,
      "text": "Write it into the schema comments so nobody 'improves' the id later.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 238.027,
      "end": 241.194,
      "text": "Improvement is how encoded keys are born.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 241.194,
      "end": 242.875,
      "text": "Refuse the improvement.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 242.875,
      "end": 244.402,
      "text": "Pick dull, and stop.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 244.402,
      "end": 246.027,
      "text": "People change names.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 246.027,
      "end": 247.513,
      "text": "They share birthdays.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 247.513,
      "end": 254.361,
      "text": "Systems that treated name plus date of birth as identity have merged two humans into one record.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 254.361,
      "end": 263.333,
      "text": "A primary key is one or more attributes that identify a row, unique, and never a second meaning hiding in the same columns.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 263.333,
      "end": 266.444,
      "text": "A table has exactly one primary key.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 266.444,
      "end": 272.736,
      "text": "A super key is any set of columns that is still unique, including extras you do not need.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 272.736,
      "end": 277.236,
      "text": "email plus city might still be unique because email already was.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 277.236,
      "end": 279.0,
      "text": "The city is surplus.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 279.122,
      "end": 286.127,
      "text": "A candidate key is a super key with no surplus: take anything away and uniqueness dies.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 286.127,
      "end": 288.099,
      "text": "email might be a candidate.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 288.099,
      "end": 289.863,
      "text": "username might be another.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 289.863,
      "end": 292.891,
      "text": "You pick one candidate to be the primary key.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 292.891,
      "end": 295.335,
      "text": "The others can be UNIQUE constraints.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 295.335,
      "end": 305.002,
      "text": "The process is: list candidates from real data, pick the stable dull one, or invent a surrogate if none of the candidates will stay still.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 305.002,
      "end": 310.849,
      "text": "Natural keys come from the world: ISBN, national identifiers, email.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 310.849,
      "end": 313.474,
      "text": "They are tempting because they look meaningful.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 313.474,
      "end": 319.572,
      "text": "They are dangerous when the world reissues them, recycles them, or lets people change them.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 319.572,
      "end": 326.363,
      "text": "A surrogate is an attribute you create, often an integer, that means only: this row.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 326.363,
      "end": 331.238,
      "text": "customer_id one hundred four is not a sentence about the business.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 331.238,
      "end": 342.044,
      "text": "AUSTIN-MAYA-2024 is a sentence, and a trap, because Austin, Maya, and 2024 can all change their jobs in that code.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 342.044,
      "end": 343.863,
      "text": "Keep story in columns.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 343.863,
      "end": 346.599,
      "text": "Keep the primary key empty of plot.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 346.599,
      "end": 354.78,
      "text": "Composite keys are honest when identity is honestly several parts: order_id plus line_number.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 354.78,
      "end": 359.808,
      "text": "They are painful as parents, because every child must copy every part.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 359.808,
      "end": 364.002,
      "text": "If many tables will point here, a small surrogate is kinder.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 364.002,
      "end": 372.947,
      "text": "Never use a value people type from memory as the only identity if a typo would attach medical or money rows to the wrong person.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 372.947,
      "end": 375.127,
      "text": "Never pick a key you will want to edit.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 375.127,
      "end": 377.294,
      "text": "Primary keys are referenced.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 377.294,
      "end": 381.405,
      "text": "Edits cascade into every child or they are refused.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 381.405,
      "end": 388.599,
      "text": "If you must expose a public code, make it UNIQUE and still keep a dull internal id as the primary key.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 388.599,
      "end": 390.599,
      "text": "Children point at the dull one.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 390.599,
      "end": 392.738,
      "text": "Humans search on the public one.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 392.738,
      "end": 395.974,
      "text": "Look at sample data before you declare uniqueness.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 395.974,
      "end": 401.294,
      "text": "Two rows that look unique in a spreadsheet of twenty can collide at twenty thousand.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 401.294,
      "end": 408.03,
      "text": "If no candidate survives contact with growth, create the new attribute and make it the primary key.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 408.03,
      "end": 410.155,
      "text": "That is not a failure of modeling.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 410.155,
      "end": 414.752,
      "text": "That is how most operational tables stay identifyable for a decade.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 414.752,
      "end": 423.488,
      "text": "Write the choice down: this is the primary key, these are the unique candidates, this is why the meaningful code is not the pointer.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 423.488,
      "end": 428.21,
      "text": "Then refuse the later improvement that encodes a city into the id.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 428.21,
      "end": 431.377,
      "text": "Improvement is how encoded keys are born.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 431.377,
      "end": 434.21,
      "text": "Pick keys for five store tables in order.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 434.21,
      "end": 440.28,
      "text": "users: candidate email, candidate username, surrogate user_id.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 440.28,
      "end": 448.919,
      "text": "Email changes, username might be recycled, so user_id primary, UNIQUE email, UNIQUE username.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 448.919,
      "end": 456.974,
      "text": "products: SKU looks natural and is reused by vendors, so product_id surrogate, UNIQUE sku.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 456.974,
      "end": 461.544,
      "text": "orders: order_id surrogate, not a date stamp code.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 461.544,
      "end": 474.058,
      "text": "reviews: review_id surrogate, because the same user can review twice if the business allows, or UNIQUE (user_id, product_id) if it does not.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 474.058,
      "end": 485.016,
      "text": "order_lines: composite (order_id, line_no) is honest, or line_id surrogate if fulfillment talks in global ids.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 485.016,
      "end": 491.516,
      "text": "Super keys would include user_id plus email; that extra email is surplus.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 491.516,
      "end": 494.919,
      "text": "Candidate keys are the minimal unique sets.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 494.919,
      "end": 497.391,
      "text": "Primary is the candidate you point at.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 497.391,
      "end": 502.155,
      "text": "The hospital parable stays in your ear: do not pick name plus birth date.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 502.155,
      "end": 504.433,
      "text": "Look at sample rows that collide.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 504.433,
      "end": 505.585,
      "text": "Then pick dull.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 505.585,
      "end": 509.224,
      "text": "Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 509.224,
      "end": 512.28,
      "text": "Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 512.28,
      "end": 513.947,
      "text": "What is a primary key?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 513.947,
      "end": 519.127,
      "text": "The candidate you pick to identify the row, unique, one per table.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 519.127,
      "end": 520.183,
      "text": "Super key?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 520.183,
      "end": 523.794,
      "text": "Any unique set, including surplus columns.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 523.794,
      "end": 524.988,
      "text": "Candidate key?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 524.988,
      "end": 526.724,
      "text": "A minimal unique set.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 526.724,
      "end": 528.474,
      "text": "Natural versus surrogate?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 528.474,
      "end": 531.655,
      "text": "From the world versus invented as this-row.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 531.655,
      "end": 533.085,
      "text": "Why dull ids?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 533.085,
      "end": 535.877,
      "text": "They do not encode a story that will change.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 535.877,
      "end": 537.363,
      "text": "Name plus birth date?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 537.363,
      "end": 539.766,
      "text": "A merging disaster waiting to happen.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 539.766,
      "end": 541.627,
      "text": "Email as primary key?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 541.627,
      "end": 544.724,
      "text": "Usually a unique handle, not the pointer.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 544.724,
      "end": 546.322,
      "text": "Composite as parent?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 546.322,
      "end": 550.974,
      "text": "Every child copies every part; often a surrogate is kinder.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 550.974,
      "end": 552.391,
      "text": "Look at sample data?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 552.391,
      "end": 555.919,
      "text": "Collisions appear at scale, not at twenty rows.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 555.919,
      "end": 558.377,
      "text": "Public code plus internal id?",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 558.377,
      "end": 561.669,
      "text": "UNIQUE the code, point children at the id.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 561.669,
      "end": 566.391,
      "text": "If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.",
      "slide": ""
    }
  ]
}
